WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:10.500
Calling Microsoft Graph or downstream APIs requires clear design for token acquisition, permissions, consent, caching, and failure handling.

2
00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:17.320
A downstream call should request the least privilege required for the scenario rather than broad permissions by default.

3
00:00:17.420 --> 00:00:25.180
Microsoft Graph permissions vary widely in sensitivity, so each requested permission should have a clear business reason and an owner.

4
00:00:25.460 --> 00:00:31.860
On-behalf-of calls must preserve delegated user context and still enforce local API authorization.

5
00:00:31.960 --> 00:00:39.320
The fact that an API can acquire a downstream token does not mean the caller may perform every local action or access every resource.

6
00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:44.540
The local API should validate its own access requirements before making downstream calls.

7
00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:49.700
Token caches should be protected according to application type and hosting environment.

8
00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:56.360
A web app, API, daemon service, desktop app, and development tool can have different cache storage needs.

9
00:00:56.460 --> 00:01:03.580
Cache behavior should be reviewed for isolation, data protection, eviction, and operational troubleshooting.

10
00:01:03.860 --> 00:01:09.620
Logs should include useful identity and authorization signals without recording sensitive values.

11
00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:23.400
Useful events may include sign-in outcomes, consent problems, authorization denials, downstream API failures, claims challenge handling, credential expiration warnings, and app registration review findings.

12
00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:32.860
Logs should not include access tokens, refresh tokens, ID tokens, authorization headers, cookies, client secrets, private keys, or raw sensitive payloads.

13
00:01:33.140 --> 00:01:50.580
Production review should cover app registrations, redirect URIs, owners, credentials, permissions, consent, tenant restrictions, issuer validation, token cache behavior, logging, alerting, incident response, and stale applications.

14
00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:54.600
Development settings should not silently become production behavior.
